{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MkuwirGvuUkWvVGSed6LxF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% === Execution Simulation ===\n2\t\n3\t@article{maimon2026selfexecution,\n4\t  title={Self-Execution Simulation Improves Coding Models},\n5\t  author={Maimon, Gallil and Yoran, Ori and Kreuk, Felix and Hassid, Michael and Cohen, Gal and Chambon, Pierre and Adi, Yossi},\n6\t  journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.03253},\n7\t  year={2026}\n8\t}\n9\t\n10\t@article{meta2025cwm,\n11\t  title={{CWM}: An Open-Weights {LLM} for Research on Code Generation with World Models},\n12\t  author={{FAIR CodeGen team} and Copet, Jade and Carbonneaux, Quentin and Cohen, Gal and Gehring, Jonas and Kahn, Jacob and Kossen, Jannik and Kreuk, Felix and McMilin, Emily and Meyer, Michel and Wei, Yuxiang and Zhang, David and Zheng, Kunhao and others},\n13\t  journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.02387},\n14\t  year={2025}\n15\t}\n16\t\n17\t@article{liu2024codemind,\n18\t  title={{CodeMind}: A Framework to Challenge Large Language Models for Code Reasoning},\n19\t  author={Liu, Changshu and Zhang, Shizhuo Dylan and Ibrahimzada, Ali Reza and Jabbarvand, Reyhaneh},\n20\t  journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.09664},\n21\t  year={2024}\n22\t}\n23\t\n24\t@article{abdollahi2025demystifying,\n25\t  title={Demystifying Errors in {LLM} Reasoning Traces: An Empirical Study of Code Execution Simulation},\n26\t  author={Abdollahi, Mohammad and Tasnia, Khandaker Rifah and Saha, Soumit Kanti and Yang, Jinqiu and Wang, Song and Hemmati, Hadi},\n27\t  journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2512.00215},\n28\t  year={2025}\n29\t}\n30\t\n31\t@article{lee2026solidcoder,\n32\t  title={{SolidCoder}: Bridging the Mental-Reality Gap in {LLM} Code Generation through Concrete Execution},\n33\t  author={Lee, Woojin and Huang, Jin-Xia},\n34\t  journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.19825},\n35\t  year={2026}\n36\t}\n37\t\n38\t% === LLM Verification for Code ===\n39\t\n40\t@article{shum2025swerm,\n41\t  title={{SWE-RM}: Execution-free Feedback for Software Engineering Agents},\n42\t  author={Shum, KaShun and Hui, Binyuan and Chen, Jiawei and Zhang, Lei and Yang, Jiaxi and Huang, Yuzhen and Lin, Junyang and He, Junxian},\n43\t  journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2512.21919},\n44\t  year={2025}\n45\t}\n46\t\n47\t@article{xu2025patchreasoner,\n48\t  title={Scalable Supervising Software Agents with Patch Reasoner},\n49\t  author={Xu, Junjielong and Tan, Boyin and Liu, Xiaoyuan and Peng, Chao and Gao, Pengfei and He, Pinjia},\n50\t  journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.22775},\n51\t  year={2025}\n52\t}\n53\t\n54\t@inproceedings{chen2025vgsearch,\n55\t  title={Rethinking Optimal Verification Granularity for Compute-Efficient Test-Time Scaling},\n56\t  author={Chen, Hao Mark and Lu, Guanxi and Okoshi, Yasuyuki and Mo, Zhiwen and Motomura, Masato and Fan, Hongxiang},\n57\t  booktitle={NeurIPS},\n58\t  year={2025}\n59\t}\n60\t\n61\t@inproceedings{snell2024scaling,\n62\t  title={Scaling {LLM} Test-Time Compute Optimally can be More Effective than Scaling Model Parameters},\n63\t  author={Snell, Charlie and Lee, Jaehoon and Xu, Kelvin and Kumar, Aviral},\n64\t  booktitle={ICLR},\n65\t  year={2025},\n66\t  note={Oral}\n67\t}\n68\t\n69\t@inproceedings{li2025sstar,\n70\t  title={S*: Test Time Scaling for Code Generation},\n71\t  author={Li, Dacheng and Cao, Shiyi and Cao, Chengkun and Li, Xiuyu and Tan, Shangyin and Keutzer, Kurt and Xing, Jiarong and Gonzalez, Joseph E. and Stoica, Ion},\n72\t  booktitle={Findings of EMNLP},\n73\t  year={2025}\n74\t}\n75\t\n76\t% === APR and SWE-bench ===\n77\t\n78\t@inproceedings{jimenez2024swebench,\n79\t  title={{SWE-bench}: Can Language Models Resolve Real-world {GitHub} Issues?},\n80\t  author={Jimenez, Carlos E and Yang, John and Wettig, Alexander and Yao, Shunyu and Pei, Kexin and Press, Ofir and Narasimhan, Karthik},\n81\t  booktitle={ICLR},\n82\t  year={2024}\n83\t}\n84\t\n85\t@misc{chowdhury2024swebenchverified,\n86\t  title={Introducing {SWE-bench} Verified},\n87\t  author={Chowdhury, Neil and Aung, James and Shern, Chan Jun and Jaffe, Oliver and Sherburn, Dane and Starace, Giulio and Mays, Evan and Dias, Rachel and Aljubeh, Marwan and Glaese, Mia and Jimenez, Carlos E. and Yang, John and Ho, Leyton and Patwardhan, Tejal and Liu, Kevin and Madry, Aleksander},\n88\t  year={2024},\n89\t  url={https://openai.com/index/introducing-swe-bench-verified/}\n90\t}\n91\t\n92\t@inproceedings{yang2024sweagent,\n93\t  title={{SWE-agent}: Agent-Computer Interfaces Enable Automated Software Engineering},\n94\t  author={Yang, John and Jimenez, Carlos E and Wettig, Alexander and Lieret, Kilian and Yao, Shunyu and Narasimhan, Karthik and Press, Ofir},\n95\t  booktitle={NeurIPS},\n96\t  year={2024}\n97\t}\n98\t\n99\t@inproceedings{xia2024agentless,\n100\t  title={Agentless: Demystifying {LLM}-based Software Engineering Agents},"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EPfDHgLy2pupwuc6aJcQjF","session_id":"a1d10b2a-da1c-4436-8566-b8bd50259b88","uuid":"d04dc0c3-4e30-4014-824a-055a32976545","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:48:44.496Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a52968ff94bf14e5d","tool_use_id":"toolu_01WN76djQWivoqzBArf7qbDa","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":34173,"tool_uses":7,"duration_ms":54865},"uuid":"1e3624e9-2b3b-43c7-b680-4716b7658728","session_id":"a1d10b2a-da1c-4436-8566-b8bd50259b88"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WN76djQWivoqzBArf7qbDa","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have sufficient information to write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Per-Test Binary Classification for Execution-Free Code Verification\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper reformulates execution-free code verification from generation (CWM) to per-test binary classification (CLS), showing that the classification advantage is complexity-dependent: negligible at function level (~1pp) but dramatic at repository level (19-26pp), where CWM suffers catastrophic format compliance collapse. The statistical methodology (clustered bootstrap, TOST equivalence) is above-average for ML papers, and the failure mode analysis of CWM is thorough. However, the repo-level results rest on dangerously few seeds, the cross-model validation is incomplete at repository scale, and several planned experiments that would strengthen the narrative were not completed.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-motivated reformulation.** The three failure modes of generation (token-budget exhaustion, string-value errors, format collapse) are clearly articulated, and the classification formulation is an elegant bypass-by-construction.\n- **Strong statistical methodology.** Clustered bootstrap with ICC-based DEFF, TOST equivalence testing, and explicit acknowledgment of within-problem correlation are methodologically rigorous for this field.\n- **Informative ablations.** The LoRA rank ablation, majority voting test, and matched-context sequence-length ablation systematically rule out alternative explanations (capacity, stochastic error, input length asymmetry).\n- **Honest limitations section.** The paper clearly flags single-seed comparisons, synthetic-to-real transfer gap, and the default-assignment artifact that inflates CWM's apparent scaling improvement.\n- **Oracle framework.** The accuracy-utility frontier with block-correlation modeling provides a principled decision criterion for when per-test verification adds value.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) Repo-level single-seed comparisons.** The headline result (25.59pp gap at 4B) rests on a single seed. The paper acknowledges seed 123 at 4B CLS gives only 75.33% (vs. 83.63% seed 42)---an 8.3pp swing---which severely undermines confidence. If CLS seed 123 were the comparison point, the gap would be ~16pp rather than ~26pp. The claim of \"19-26pp\" masks massive seed variance at repo level.\n\n- **(Major) Missing cross-model repo-level validation.** DeepSeek-Coder repo-level CLS and CWM are listed as \"in progress\" with dashes in the table. At function level, DeepSeek shows CLS-CWM gap of 0.02pp (vs. Qwen 1.24pp), suggesting model-specific behavior. Without repo-level cross-model data, the central complexity-dependent claim could be specific to Qwen3's architecture or tokenizer.\n\n- **(Major) Absent DeepSeek TRAJ experiments.** Planned DeepSeek repo-level TRAJ and the fixed func-level TRAJ (resolving data leakage) were never completed. The CLS-TRAJ equivalence claim therefore rests entirely on Qwen3. If DeepSeek shows CLS significantly differs from TRAJ, the \"free diagnostic granularity\" claim collapses.\n\n- **(Moderate) CWM baseline is not the published CWM.** The paper fine-tunes small LoRA adapters on 4-8B models and calls this \"CWM,\" but the original Code World Models paper uses 32B+ models specifically trained for execution simulation. The comparison is thus against a weak strawman. The paper notes this but still titles itself as comparing against \"Code World Models.\"\n\n- **(Moderate) No downstream re-repair validation.** The conclusion claims per-test signals \"unlock targeted re-repair,\" but no experiment validates this. The re-repair experiment from the plan (Table 5 in the summary) shows CLS-Diag only +1.0pp over control (p=0.62)---not significant. If present in the paper, this would undermine the practical utility claim.\n\n- **(Minor) Synthetic training data.** All models are trained on mutation-generated data. Transfer to real-world developer bugs is untested, limiting practical applicability claims.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n- What is CLS seed 123 accuracy at 4B repo level with the val-best checkpoint at epoch 3 (not step 800)? The 75.33% figure suggests checkpoint selection, not the formulation, may drive the gap.\n- Can you provide even a partial DeepSeek repo-level result (single seed) to confirm the gap is not Qwen-specific?\n- Why does CWM parseable-only accuracy at repo level fall below random (38-43%)? Is the model actively learning harmful patterns, or is there a label leakage/data processing issue?\n- The CWM training uses max_seq_length=2048 but repo-level inputs are much longer---is the CWM being compared fairly, or is it trained on truncated contexts that make repo-level generation impossible by design?\n\n### Missing Experiments Assessment\n\nFour planned experiments were not completed: DeepSeek repo-level TRAJ (2 seeds) and fixed DeepSeek func-level TRAJ (2 seeds, data leakage resolution). These are critical because: (1) the CLS-TRAJ equivalence is the paper's second contribution, currently supported by only one model family; (2) the data leakage bug means the existing DeepSeek func-level TRAJ comparison in Table 4 is unreliable. Additionally, the DeepSeek repo-level CLS/CWM entries are blank. Together, these gaps mean the cross-model validation story---essential for generality claims---is incomplete at the complexity level where it matters most.\n\n### Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: High\n\nThe core idea is sound and the function-level experiments are well-executed, but the repo-level results that carry the paper's central \"complexity-dependent\" narrative are under-seeded, single-model, and lack the cross-model validation needed to support general claims. Completing the missing experiments could elevate this to a borderline accept."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a52968ff94bf14e5d (use SendMessage with to: 'a52968ff94bf14e5d' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 35398\ntool_uses: 7\nduration_ms: 54867</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a1d10b2a-da1c-4436-8566-b8bd50259b88","uuid":"d43a924a-80ff-4009-9986-101789af4d8a","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:49:07.377Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. Review the paper below using a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept; acceptance threshold ~25%).\n\n# Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Per-Test Binary Classification for Execution-Free Code Verification: A Complexity-Dependent Reformulation\n\n**Core idea:** Reformulate execution-free code verification as per-test binary classification (CLS)—predict pass/fail per test without generating execution traces. Compare against Code World Models (CWM, generation-based) and trajectory-level scoring (TRAJ, single binary label per patch).\n\n**Dataset:** SWE-bench-PerTest — 438 instances, 87,600 test-level labels, 16 candidates each, 73/27 pass/fail ratio.\n\n**Key results:**\n- Table 2 (Repo-level CLS): Qwen3-4B 83.63%, Qwen3-8B 84.79%, DeepSeek-V2-Lite 74.20%, majority baseline 72.96%. Qwen F1 metrics are MISSING (dashes in table). DeepSeek: F1_macro=67.8, F1_pass=82.5, F1_fail=53.1.\n- Table 3 (Repo-level CWM): Qwen3-8B v12 unparseable=53.7%, v10=93%; DeepSeek unparseable=28%, overall acc=62.8%, parseable acc=66.3%. CLS-CWM gap: >15pp Qwen, 11.4pp DeepSeek.\n- Table 4 (DeepSeek func-level): CLS 86.93%, TRAJ seed42 83.24%, TRAJ seed123 85.08%. Only N=2 seeds. Caveat about unfixed evaluation pipeline with potential data leakage.\n- Section 3.5 (CLS≈TRAJ Qwen3): Pooled gap −0.40pp, 95% CI [−1.32, +0.52], TOST p=0.007, ±2pp margin. 5 seeds, 114 clusters, 10K bootstrap resamples. ICC=0.191, DEFF=3.97.\n- Table 5 (Re-repair): Control 0.215, CLS-Diag 0.225 (+1.0pp, p=0.62), Oracle-Diag 0.285 (+7.0pp), Random-Diag 0.175 (−4.0pp). N=200 instances, 5 repair attempts each.\n\n**Missing experiments acknowledged:**\n- DeepSeek repo-level TRAJ (2 seeds): not completed\n- DeepSeek func-level TRAJ (fixed evaluation): data leakage bug unresolved\n\n**Experiment design reference (from review_context.yaml):**\nThe planned experiments included: diagnostic-rerepair, swebench-pertest-data, deepseek-cls-repo, deepseek-cwm-repo, deepseek-traj-repo (NOT COMPLETED), deepseek-traj-repo-s123 (NOT COMPLETED), deepseek-traj-func-v2-fixed (FAILED_TO_RESOLVE), deepseek-traj-func-v2-s123-fixed (FAILED_TO_RESOLVE).\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Methodological soundness** — Are the formulations well-defined? Are comparisons controlled and fair? Is the statistical methodology appropriate?\n2. **Experimental rigor** — Are there enough seeds/runs? Are baselines adequate? Are the missing experiments critical? Is the data leakage concern adequately addressed?\n3. **Results interpretation** — Are claims appropriately scoped? Do the numbers support the claims? Are caveats sufficient?\n4. **Reproducibility** — Is there enough detail to reproduce?\n\nProvide:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: minor/moderate/major/critical)\n- Questions for Authors (bulleted)\n- Missing Experiments Assessment: which planned experiments are missing and how much this hurts\n- Score (1-6) with confidence (low/medium/high)\n- Short recommendation sentence\n\nKeep your review under 800 words.","agentId":"a52968ff94bf14e5d","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have sufficient information to write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Per-Test Binary Classification for Execution-Free Code Verification\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper reformulates execution-free code verification from generation (CWM) to per-test binary classification (CLS), showing that the classification advantage is complexity-dependent: negligible at function level (~1pp) but dramatic at repository level (19-26pp), where CWM suffers catastrophic format compliance collapse. The statistical methodology (clustered bootstrap, TOST equivalence) is above-average for ML papers, and the failure mode analysis of CWM is thorough. However, the repo-level results rest on dangerously few seeds, the cross-model validation is incomplete at repository scale, and several planned experiments that would strengthen the narrative were not completed.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-motivated reformulation.** The three failure modes of generation (token-budget exhaustion, string-value errors, format collapse) are clearly articulated, and the classification formulation is an elegant bypass-by-construction.\n- **Strong statistical methodology.** Clustered bootstrap with ICC-based DEFF, TOST equivalence testing, and explicit acknowledgment of within-problem correlation are methodologically rigorous for this field.\n- **Informative ablations.** The LoRA rank ablation, majority voting test, and matched-context sequence-length ablation systematically rule out alternative explanations (capacity, stochastic error, input length asymmetry).\n- **Honest limitations section.** The paper clearly flags single-seed comparisons, synthetic-to-real transfer gap, and the default-assignment artifact that inflates CWM's apparent scaling improvement.\n- **Oracle framework.** The accuracy-utility frontier with block-correlation modeling provides a principled decision criterion for when per-test verification adds value.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) Repo-level single-seed comparisons.** The headline result (25.59pp gap at 4B) rests on a single seed. The paper acknowledges seed 123 at 4B CLS gives only 75.33% (vs. 83.63% seed 42)---an 8.3pp swing---which severely undermines confidence. If CLS seed 123 were the comparison point, the gap would be ~16pp rather than ~26pp. The claim of \"19-26pp\" masks massive seed variance at repo level.\n\n- **(Major) Missing cross-model repo-level validation.** DeepSeek-Coder repo-level CLS and CWM are listed as \"in progress\" with dashes in the table. At function level, DeepSeek shows CLS-CWM gap of 0.02pp (vs. Qwen 1.24pp), suggesting model-specific behavior. Without repo-level cross-model data, the central complexity-dependent claim could be specific to Qwen3's architecture or tokenizer.\n\n- **(Major) Absent DeepSeek TRAJ experiments.** Planned DeepSeek repo-level TRAJ and the fixed func-level TRAJ (resolving data leakage) were never completed. The CLS-TRAJ equivalence claim therefore rests entirely on Qwen3. If DeepSeek shows CLS significantly differs from TRAJ, the \"free diagnostic granularity\" claim collapses.\n\n- **(Moderate) CWM baseline is not the published CWM.** The paper fine-tunes small LoRA adapters on 4-8B models and calls this \"CWM,\" but the original Code World Models paper uses 32B+ models specifically trained for execution simulation. The comparison is thus against a weak strawman. The paper notes this but still titles itself as comparing against \"Code World Models.\"\n\n- **(Moderate) No downstream re-repair validation.** The conclusion claims per-test signals \"unlock targeted re-repair,\" but no experiment validates this. The re-repair experiment from the plan (Table 5 in the summary) shows CLS-Diag only +1.0pp over control (p=0.62)---not significant. If present in the paper, this would undermine the practical utility claim.\n\n- **(Minor) Synthetic training data.** All models are trained on mutation-generated data. Transfer to real-world developer bugs is untested, limiting practical applicability claims.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n- What is CLS seed 123 accuracy at 4B repo level with the val-best checkpoint at epoch 3 (not step 800)? The 75.33% figure suggests checkpoint selection, not the formulation, may drive the gap.\n- Can you provide even a partial DeepSeek repo-level result (single seed) to confirm the gap is not Qwen-specific?\n- Why does CWM parseable-only accuracy at repo level fall below random (38-43%)? Is the model actively learning harmful patterns, or is there a label leakage/data processing issue?\n- The CWM training uses max_seq_length=2048 but repo-level inputs are much longer---is the CWM being compared fairly, or is it trained on truncated contexts that make repo-level generation impossible by design?\n\n### Missing Experiments Assessment\n\nFour planned experiments were not completed: DeepSeek repo-level TRAJ (2 seeds) and fixed DeepSeek func-level TRAJ (2 seeds, data leakage resolution). These are critical because: (1) the CLS-TRAJ equivalence is the paper's second contribution, currently supported by only one model family; (2) the data leakage bug means the existing DeepSeek func-level TRAJ comparison in Table 4 is unreliable. Additionally, the DeepSeek repo-level CLS/CWM entries are blank. Together, these gaps mean the cross-model validation story---essential for generality claims---is incomplete at the complexity level where it matters most.\n\n### Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: High\n\nThe core idea is sound and the function-level experiments are well-executed, but the repo-level results that carry the paper's central \"complexity-dependent\" narrative are under-seeded, single-model, and lack the cross-model validation needed to support general claims. Completing the missing experiments could elevate this to a borderline accept."}],"totalDurationMs":54867,"totalTokens":35398,"totalToolUseCount":7,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2473,"cache_read_input_tokens":31498,"output_tokens":1426,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2473},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":1426,"cache_read_input_tokens":31498,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2473,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2473,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":7,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
