{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EPfDHgLy2pupwuc6aJcQjF","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have sufficient information to write a thorough review. Let me compile my assessment.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Per-Test Binary Classification for Execution-Free Code Verification\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper proposes reformulating execution-free code verification from output-string generation (CWM) to per-test binary pass/fail classification (CLS). The key claim is that this advantage is \"complexity-dependent\": negligible at function-level but critical at repository-level, where CWM suffers format compliance collapse. The paper also shows CLS matches trajectory-level accuracy while preserving per-test diagnostic granularity, and contributes an oracle framework characterizing when per-test signals provide value.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-articulated failure mode analysis.** The three CWM failure modes (token-budget exhaustion, string-value errors, format compliance collapse) are clearly characterized and empirically validated. The observation that parseable-only accuracy *decreases* with scale while overall accuracy *increases* due to default assignment is a genuinely insightful diagnostic.\n\n- **Controlled experimental design.** Matched seeds, same training data, same LoRA configuration across formulations. The function-level experiments with 5 seeds and clustered bootstrap provide credible statistical grounding. The TOST equivalence test for CLS vs. TRAJ is methodologically appropriate.\n\n- **Oracle accuracy-utility framework.** The ICC-based block-correlation model (Eq. 2-3) provides a principled characterization of when per-test granularity helps, with testable predictions validated by data. The finding that advantage concentrates at T>=2 F2P tests is practically useful.\n\n- **Thorough ablations and self-critical limitations.** The matched-context ablation ruling out input length as confound, the relaxed-matching experiment showing CWM cannot be rescued, and the LoRA rank/majority voting ablations all strengthen the core argument. The limitations section is commendably honest.\n\n- **Inference efficiency analysis.** The 217x throughput advantage is practically significant for deployment.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) Limited novelty of the core idea.** Per-test binary classification is a straightforward simplification of the generation task. The paper acknowledges that LEVER and CodeT already use classification-based verification; the distinction drawn (per-test diagnostics vs. candidate-level ranking) is real but narrow. The \"reformulation\" amounts to predicting a single token instead of a string — an obvious baseline that any practitioner would try. The contribution is more empirical than methodological.\n\n- **(Major) Single-architecture primary evidence.** The central \"complexity-dependent\" claim at repo-level rests entirely on Qwen3. DeepSeek-Coder cross-model validation exists only at function-level (where the gap is already negligible). The paper's own title/abstract/claims generalize broadly, but the repo-level evidence is model-specific, single-seed (4B) or two-point (8B). The \"model-dependent\" caveat is buried in limitations rather than foregrounded in the abstract/claims.\n\n- **(Major) CWM baseline is not representative.** The authors explicitly state their CWM is a \"LoRA fine-tuned variant for controlled comparison, not a reproduction of the 32B Code World Model.\" A 4B LoRA model attempting generation on repo-level contexts is an extremely weak baseline for generation — it would be surprising if it *didn't* fail catastrophically. The original CWM uses 32B parameters specifically because generation demands more capacity. The controlled comparison sacrifices ecological validity: the paper shows classification beats an underpowered generator, not that classification beats generation as practiced.\n\n- **(Moderate) Broken citations throughout.** All references render as (?) in the PDF per the task summary. This is a critical presentation failure for a venue submission — reviewers cannot verify claims against literature.\n\n- **(Moderate) No downstream task validation.** The re-repair experiment (Table 5 in the summary) shows CLS-Diag at +1.0pp with p=0.62 — not significant. The paper's practical motivation (targeted re-repair from per-test diagnostics) is unvalidated. The diagnostic granularity is \"free\" only in an information-theoretic sense; it has no demonstrated utility.\n\n- **(Minor) Missing related work.** No discussion of test-case-aware reward models from RLHF literature (e.g., unit-test-level feedback for code generation). No comparison to LLM-as-verifier approaches beyond zero-shot baselines. The VG-Search citation is close to this paper's core question (verification granularity for scaling) but receives only surface treatment.\n\n- **(Minor) Dataset contribution understated/unclear.** SWE-bench-PerTest (438 instances, 87K labels) is mentioned as a resource contribution but construction details, licensing, and release plans are not evident from the main text.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n- How would a 32B CWM (or even an 8B model with full fine-tuning rather than LoRA) perform at repo-level? The controlled comparison is appreciated, but can you argue the failure modes persist at adequate generation capacity?\n\n- The CWM unparseable rate varies from near-zero (scikit-learn) to 100% (sphinx). Does CLS show similar per-repository variance in accuracy? If so, does the gap shrink for \"easy\" repositories?\n\n- Why not include a hybrid baseline (CLS when CWM output is unparseable, CWM when parseable) as a simple ensemble? The error complementarity analysis (90.63% oracle) suggests this could be competitive.\n\n- Can you provide any evidence that the synthetic mutation distribution is representative of real SWE-bench patches?\n\n### Score and Recommendation\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**  \n**Confidence: High**\n\nThe paper presents a thorough empirical study with sound statistical methodology, but the core contribution — replacing generation with classification — is an obvious simplification rather than a novel insight. The strongest claims (repo-level dominance) rest on an underpowered CWM baseline and single-model evidence, while the promised downstream utility (re-repair) is unvalidated. Fix the citations, complete cross-model repo-level experiments, and either validate re-repair or reframe contributions around the empirical characterization and oracle framework."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a882cd6771d736408 (use SendMessage with to: 'a882cd6771d736408' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 33987\ntool_uses: 8\nduration_ms: 59416</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a1d10b2a-da1c-4436-8566-b8bd50259b88","uuid":"567b82f7-c6eb-4201-ba48-c5f64c7239fc","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:49:27.404Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **novelty, framing, and related work**. Review the paper below using a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept; acceptance threshold ~25%).\n\n# Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Per-Test Binary Classification for Execution-Free Code Verification: A Complexity-Dependent Reformulation\n\n**Core idea:** Reformulate execution-free code verification as per-test binary classification (CLS)—predict pass/fail per test without generating execution traces. Compare against Code World Models (CWM, generation-based) and trajectory-level scoring (TRAJ, single binary label per patch).\n\n**Dataset:** SWE-bench-PerTest — 438 instances, 87,600 test-level labels, 16 candidates each, 73/27 pass/fail ratio.\n\n**Claims:**\n1. Complexity-dependent reformulation advantage: CLS and CWM equivalent at function-level, but CLS scales better to repo-level (11–15+ pp gap, model-dependent).\n2. Diagnostic granularity at equivalent accuracy: CLS≈TRAJ within Qwen3 (TOST p=0.007, ±2pp margin). Cross-architecture validation is future work.\n3. Accuracy-utility frontier: Re-repair with oracle diagnostics helps (+7pp), but current classifier precision (~83%) insufficient—threshold ~90% needed.\n4. Resource contribution: SWE-bench-PerTest dataset.\n\n**Related Work section covers:**\n- Execution-free code verification: SWE-RewardModel, Code World Models\n- Per-test prediction: test case prioritization in CI, flaky test detection\n- LLM-based code reasoning: LLMs reasoning about code execution\n\n**Missing experiments:**\n- DeepSeek repo-level TRAJ (not completed)\n- DeepSeek func-level TRAJ fixed evaluation (data leakage unresolved)\n- All references show as (?) — citations are broken/missing in the PDF\n\n**Key numbers:**\n- Table 2: Qwen3 CLS repo-level 83-85%, DeepSeek 74.2%, majority 72.96%. Qwen F1 metrics missing from table.\n- Table 3: CWM unparseable rates 28% (DeepSeek) to 53-93% (Qwen). CLS-CWM gap 11-15+pp.\n- Table 5: Re-repair CLS-Diag +1.0pp (p=0.62, not significant), Oracle +7.0pp.\n- CLS≈TRAJ: TOST p=0.007, 5 seeds, Qwen only.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty** — Is per-test binary classification genuinely new? Is the \"complexity-dependent reformulation\" framing justified by the evidence? How incremental vs. substantial is the contribution?\n2. **Related work coverage** — Are key baselines and prior work adequately cited and compared? What's missing?\n3. **Framing and claims** — Are claims well-scoped? Is the title/abstract overselling relative to evidence? Is the \"model-dependent\" caveat prominent enough?\n4. **Significance and impact** — Would this paper advance the field? Is the dataset contribution sufficient?\n5. **Presentation quality** — Note the broken citations (?? throughout). Missing F1 values in Table 2. Overall clarity.\n\nProvide:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: minor/moderate/major/critical)\n- Questions for Authors (bulleted)\n- Score (1-6) with confidence (low/medium/high)\n- Short recommendation sentence\n\nKeep your review under 800 words.","agentId":"a882cd6771d736408","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have sufficient information to write a thorough review. Let me compile my assessment.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Per-Test Binary Classification for Execution-Free Code Verification\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper proposes reformulating execution-free code verification from output-string generation (CWM) to per-test binary pass/fail classification (CLS). The key claim is that this advantage is \"complexity-dependent\": negligible at function-level but critical at repository-level, where CWM suffers format compliance collapse. The paper also shows CLS matches trajectory-level accuracy while preserving per-test diagnostic granularity, and contributes an oracle framework characterizing when per-test signals provide value.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-articulated failure mode analysis.** The three CWM failure modes (token-budget exhaustion, string-value errors, format compliance collapse) are clearly characterized and empirically validated. The observation that parseable-only accuracy *decreases* with scale while overall accuracy *increases* due to default assignment is a genuinely insightful diagnostic.\n\n- **Controlled experimental design.** Matched seeds, same training data, same LoRA configuration across formulations. The function-level experiments with 5 seeds and clustered bootstrap provide credible statistical grounding. The TOST equivalence test for CLS vs. TRAJ is methodologically appropriate.\n\n- **Oracle accuracy-utility framework.** The ICC-based block-correlation model (Eq. 2-3) provides a principled characterization of when per-test granularity helps, with testable predictions validated by data. The finding that advantage concentrates at T>=2 F2P tests is practically useful.\n\n- **Thorough ablations and self-critical limitations.** The matched-context ablation ruling out input length as confound, the relaxed-matching experiment showing CWM cannot be rescued, and the LoRA rank/majority voting ablations all strengthen the core argument. The limitations section is commendably honest.\n\n- **Inference efficiency analysis.** The 217x throughput advantage is practically significant for deployment.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) Limited novelty of the core idea.** Per-test binary classification is a straightforward simplification of the generation task. The paper acknowledges that LEVER and CodeT already use classification-based verification; the distinction drawn (per-test diagnostics vs. candidate-level ranking) is real but narrow. The \"reformulation\" amounts to predicting a single token instead of a string — an obvious baseline that any practitioner would try. The contribution is more empirical than methodological.\n\n- **(Major) Single-architecture primary evidence.** The central \"complexity-dependent\" claim at repo-level rests entirely on Qwen3. DeepSeek-Coder cross-model validation exists only at function-level (where the gap is already negligible). The paper's own title/abstract/claims generalize broadly, but the repo-level evidence is model-specific, single-seed (4B) or two-point (8B). The \"model-dependent\" caveat is buried in limitations rather than foregrounded in the abstract/claims.\n\n- **(Major) CWM baseline is not representative.** The authors explicitly state their CWM is a \"LoRA fine-tuned variant for controlled comparison, not a reproduction of the 32B Code World Model.\" A 4B LoRA model attempting generation on repo-level contexts is an extremely weak baseline for generation — it would be surprising if it *didn't* fail catastrophically. The original CWM uses 32B parameters specifically because generation demands more capacity. The controlled comparison sacrifices ecological validity: the paper shows classification beats an underpowered generator, not that classification beats generation as practiced.\n\n- **(Moderate) Broken citations throughout.** All references render as (?) in the PDF per the task summary. This is a critical presentation failure for a venue submission — reviewers cannot verify claims against literature.\n\n- **(Moderate) No downstream task validation.** The re-repair experiment (Table 5 in the summary) shows CLS-Diag at +1.0pp with p=0.62 — not significant. The paper's practical motivation (targeted re-repair from per-test diagnostics) is unvalidated. The diagnostic granularity is \"free\" only in an information-theoretic sense; it has no demonstrated utility.\n\n- **(Minor) Missing related work.** No discussion of test-case-aware reward models from RLHF literature (e.g., unit-test-level feedback for code generation). No comparison to LLM-as-verifier approaches beyond zero-shot baselines. The VG-Search citation is close to this paper's core question (verification granularity for scaling) but receives only surface treatment.\n\n- **(Minor) Dataset contribution understated/unclear.** SWE-bench-PerTest (438 instances, 87K labels) is mentioned as a resource contribution but construction details, licensing, and release plans are not evident from the main text.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n- How would a 32B CWM (or even an 8B model with full fine-tuning rather than LoRA) perform at repo-level? The controlled comparison is appreciated, but can you argue the failure modes persist at adequate generation capacity?\n\n- The CWM unparseable rate varies from near-zero (scikit-learn) to 100% (sphinx). Does CLS show similar per-repository variance in accuracy? If so, does the gap shrink for \"easy\" repositories?\n\n- Why not include a hybrid baseline (CLS when CWM output is unparseable, CWM when parseable) as a simple ensemble? The error complementarity analysis (90.63% oracle) suggests this could be competitive.\n\n- Can you provide any evidence that the synthetic mutation distribution is representative of real SWE-bench patches?\n\n### Score and Recommendation\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**  \n**Confidence: High**\n\nThe paper presents a thorough empirical study with sound statistical methodology, but the core contribution — replacing generation with classification — is an obvious simplification rather than a novel insight. The strongest claims (repo-level dominance) rest on an underpowered CWM baseline and single-model evidence, while the promised downstream utility (re-repair) is unvalidated. Fix the citations, complete cross-model repo-level experiments, and either validate re-repair or reframe contributions around the empirical characterization and oracle framework."}],"totalDurationMs":59416,"totalTokens":33987,"totalToolUseCount":8,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2904,"cache_read_input_tokens":29639,"output_tokens":1443,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2904},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":1443,"cache_read_input_tokens":29639,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2904,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2904,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":8,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WpfMPcE96Qen6aTajJN1Hg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Now I need to conduct the roundtable:\n- Step 1: Independent reviews (done above)\n- Step 2: Cross-sharing — identify areas of agreement and disagreement\n- Step 3: Convergence — synthesize into a unified assessment\n- Step 4: Final report\n\nLet me synthesize the reviews and produce the final report, then submit it.\n\nKey points of agreement:\n- Both give score 3 (Weak Reject)\n- Both flag: underpowered CWM baseline, single-model repo-level evidence, missing DeepSeek TRAJ experiments, re-repair utility unvalidated\n\n\n- Both praise the statistical methodology, honest limitations, and controlled design\n\nWhere they diverge: R1 emphasizes the seed variance issue with an 8.3 percentage point swing in repo-level CLS, while R2 is more concerned about the novelty being an obvious simplification and treats the broken citations as a critical presentation failure. Now I'll draft the full roundtable report and submit it.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5188,"cache_read_input_tokens":42345,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5188},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a1d10b2a-da1c-4436-8566-b8bd50259b88","uuid":"e3bed16a-a350-45f4-8c44-d1e3da6b3aae"}
